{"author":"John Wesley","book":"Notes on the Bible","chapter":804,"classification":{"bucket":"Christian Classics / Commentaries","canonicalStatus":"noncanonical","contentKind":"commentary","cautionReason":"","doctrinalStatus":"historical","labels":["christian-classics","commentaries"],"severity":0},"id":"classics:wesley:Ps.67","lineage":[],"metadata":{"author":"John Wesley","authorshipDateRange":"18th century","genre":"Commentaries","tradition":"Methodist","translator":null,"edition":"CCEL edition; source omits 2 Samuel 24","rights":"Public Domain","source":{"id":"wesley","provider":"CCEL","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"}},"originalBook":"Psalms","sourceVolumeId":"wesley","source":{"id":"wesley","sourceUrl":"https://www.ccel.org/ccel/wesley/notes.html","title":"Notes on the Bible"},"title":"Psalms 67","reference":"Psalms 67","notes":[],"verses":[{"verse":1,"text":"A prayer for Israel, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":2,"text":"1.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":3,"text":"For the conversion of the Gentiles, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":4,"text":"2-5.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":5,"text":"A prospect of glorious times, ver.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":6,"text":"6, 7.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":7,"text":"To the chief musician on Neginoth, A psalm or song.","sourceParagraph":1},{"verse":8,"text":"2.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":9,"text":"Thy way - The way of truth, or the true religion; the same which in the next clause is called his saving health, and both together signify the way of salvation; deal so graciously with thy people, that thereby the Gentile-world may at last be allured to join with them.","sourceParagraph":2,"scriptureVerseStart":2,"scriptureVerseEnd":2},{"verse":10,"text":"4.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":11,"text":"Judge - Rule them.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":12,"text":"Govern - Hebrew. lead; gently, as a shepherd doth his sheep; and not rule them with rigor, as other lords had done.","sourceParagraph":3,"scriptureVerseStart":4,"scriptureVerseEnd":4},{"verse":13,"text":"6.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":14,"text":"Them - When the people of the earth shall be converted to God, God will cause it to yield them abundance of all sorts of fruits.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":15,"text":"Under which one blessing, all other blessings both temporal and spiritual are comprehended.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6},{"verse":16,"text":"Our own - He who is Israel's God in a peculiar manner.","sourceParagraph":4,"scriptureVerseStart":6,"scriptureVerseEnd":6}],"scriptureReference":{"book":"Psalms","chapter":67}}